<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Memory of the World Programme</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Memory of the World Register</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Memory of the World Programme is an initiative launched by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1992 to protect the world's documentary heritage and make it accessible. Its international register lists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, archives, books, films and recordings judged to be of world significance, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-the-joseon-dynasty">Annals of the Joseon Dynasty</a> and the surviving volumes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheca-corviniana">Bibliotheca Corviniana</a>. Regional and national registers support the same aim.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/memory-of-the-world-programme.jpg" alt="Memory of the World Programme" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UNESCO · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unesco_Memory_of_the_World_Logo_(English).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> began the programme in 1992 to preserve and promote documentary heritage, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, maps, rock inscriptions, court documents and diplomatic exchanges, judged to be of such global significance that they transcend the boundaries of time and culture. The programme seeks to safeguard documentary heritage against collective amnesia, neglect, decay and deliberate destruction. Its International Advisory Committee met in an interim capacity from 1993 and created the International Register at its second meeting in 1995. The first inscriptions followed in 1997, after the statutes that made the committee permanent took effect.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Any organisation or individual may propose an item through a UNESCO Member State's National Commission, and two proposals per state are considered in each nomination cycle, with no limit on joint proposals. Items are recommended by the International Advisory Committee, whose 14 members are appointed by UNESCO's Director-General, and endorsed by the Director-General, using the criteria of world significance and outstanding universal value. The register includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, oral traditions, audio-visual materials and library and archive holdings, among them recordings of folk music, landmark films and the lifetime works of writers and composers. The committee has subsidiary bodies for the register, preservation, and education and research. National and regional committees are autonomous and made up of local heritage professionals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>National and regional committees keep their own registers for heritage of national or regional importance, such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, and there are regional registers for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and the Pacific, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The Jikji Prize, established in 2004 with the South Korean government to mark the 2001 inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s Jikji, honours institutions that preserve and open documentary heritage; its first recipient, in 2005, was the Czech National Library. The audiovisual concern goes back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s 1980 Recommendation for the Safeguarding and Preservation of Moving Images. In 1995 UNESCO published its first Memory of the World list, on national cinematic heritage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Memory of the World Programme” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Annals of the Joseon Dynasty and the Diaries of the Royal Secretariat are among the most detailed state records of any premodern country and are inscribed on UNESCO's <b>Memory of the World</b> register.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Visegrád was one of the first centres of the Italian Renaissance outside Italy: Italian humanists, architects and artists worked there, and the royal library, the Bibliotheca Corviniana, held perhaps two thousand volumes, of which over two hundred survive and are listed in UNESCO's <b>Memory of the World</b> register.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Demotte Shahnameh was produced under the Ilkhanid sultan Abu Sa'id; three of Timur's grandsons, Baysunghur, Ibrahim Sultan and Mohammad Juki, each commissioned a copy, and the Baysonghori Shahnameh of 1430 is preserved intact at Golestan Palace in Iran, inscribed in UNESCO's <b>Memory of the World Register</b> in 2007.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
